# Logistics Data Analysis and Predictive Optimization

## Project Overview

This project focuses on applying data science and Python techniques to analyze logistics and supply chain operations.

The project aims to identify delivery performance patterns, analyze operational bottlenecks, predict delivery risks, and propose optimization strategies for improving supply chain efficiency.

## Objectives

- Analyze logistics and supply chain performance.
- Measure important logistics KPIs.
- Clean and preprocess logistics data.
- Perform exploratory data analysis (EDA).
- Create meaningful data visualizations.
- Identify factors associated with delivery delays.
- Develop predictive models for logistics performance.
- Propose optimization strategies for better resource allocation.

## Key Performance Indicators

The project will focus on:

1. On-Time Delivery Rate
2. Late Delivery Rate
3. Average Delivery Delay
4. Average Shipping Cost
5. Order Profitability

## Technologies Used

- Python
- Pandas
- NumPy
- Matplotlib
- Scikit-learn
- Jupyter Notebook
- GitHub

## Project Workflow

The project will be completed in four stages:

### Week 1 – Strategic Planning
Define the logistics problem, KPIs, dataset, data science methods, and analytical roadmap.

### Week 2 – Data Collection and Preprocessing
Collect the logistics dataset and perform data cleaning, missing-value handling, outlier detection, and preprocessing.

### Week 3 – Data Analysis and Visualization
Perform exploratory data analysis, calculate statistics, identify correlations, and create visualizations.

### Week 4 – Predictive Modeling and Optimization
Develop a predictive model for logistics performance and propose optimization strategies based on analytical findings.

## Dataset
The project uses the publicly available DataCo Smart Supply Chain dataset as the reference dataset.
The dataset contains logistics-related information such as shipping modes, scheduled and actual shipping days, delivery status, order details, customer information, sales, and profit-related variables.

